[Music]  exoplanets more than anything else in  the universe are really places  we know and love the planets in our  solar system and i think it's such a  beautiful thing to be able to explore  these other worlds around distant stars  exoplanet science has really exploded  over the last 25 years we went from not  knowing of any planets outside the solar  system to now knowing over four thousand  [Music]  almost every star has at least one  planet  and there are a lot of planets out there  that may not be so different from our  own earth  most of them are very very nearby so in  the scheme of things in the big picture  of the galaxy they're right next door  for me the biggest question is where  does earth fit in in the universe are we  special are there a lot of other planets  like earth or not and in my work i study  the atmospheres of exoplanets so i'm  interested in what kinds of chemicals  are in the atmosphere what their  climates are like their weather  we see a lot of pretty pictures from  other types of astronomy but for  exoplanets we usually don't see them so  we find evidence for them like if a star  gets a little bit fainter that could be  from a planet passing in front  we can detect that drop in brightness  and use that to tell us what size the  planet is at different wavelengths and  that tells us what the atmosphere is  made out of  you have to be very imaginative to take  the things that we can measure and turn  that into information about the planets  exoplanets are  always surprising i think we have a  pretty limited imagination based on the  planets of our solar system and so every  time we look at an exoplanet we see  something that is unexpected  my most surprising discovery was very  unusual clouds on a mini neptune planet  they're probably clouds that are made  out of minerals like potassium chloride  or zinc sulfide so totally different  from anything in the solar system  i think it's safe to say that we're  really in a golden age of astronomy  right now and a lot of that is driven by  technology so we're building telescopes  that are bigger and more sensitive than  ever before particularly in space for  the type of work that i do studying the  atmospheres of other planets it's a lot  easier to do this when earth's own  atmosphere is not in the way  [Music]  [Music]  i feel so incredibly lucky that i get to  use this telescope because it was really  it was first talked about in the 1990s  when i was just a kid it's been many  many many years in the making it's the  successor to hubble so it'll be in space  james webb is really a dream come true  for exoplanet science as much as i love  hubble which i've used for my work a lot  james webb will take us many steps  forward it will have observing  capability that we've never had before  in all of human history  it will allow us to answer questions  that are really important for  understanding what our place in the  universe is  we know that rocky planets are common  all over the galaxy but what are they  like are they nice environments for life  to evolve on or not james webb will make  huge progress in answering those  questions  [Music]  the way that i approach looking for an  inhabited planet is to study what its  atmosphere is made out of  if you looked at the earth from far away  you would see oxygen and methane in our  own atmosphere and from a chemistry  standpoint that is really strange  because normally those two molecules  will react with each other to form  something else  and so that's the smoking gun evidence  on the earth that something  is producing both oxygen and methane and  our best explanation for what that is is  life  i am an optimist i definitely think  there's alien life out there  we know that there are tens of billions  of rocky planets in our galaxy alone  many of them may have water  the universe is old so if you wait  around long enough if you have all the  ingredients that you need to make life i  think it's only a matter of time  before it happens  as a scientist i try to be  rational and objective about things but  it's such an emotional  topic  if i imagine myself being the first  person to find evidence for life on  another planet i'd probably be in tears  i mean i think to know something so big  that we're not alone would be an  incredible feeling  [Music]  [Music]  you

